this next story is sponsored by  intermountain healthcare and it's a tear  jerker it's about the gift of friendship  and life take a look  hi my name is rakita sanchez  i work for a las vegas police department  and i've been employed for 13 years  and i'm christina martinez and i've been  employed by the las vegas police officer  for seven years when i was 22 years old  i was diagnosed with lupus a rare form  i was told that i would probably not  make it to my 23rd birthday  i am now 37. at one point my kidneys  completely failed  they were amazed that i even walked into  the hospital because my kidney function  was below 5  the doctors told me  that i would need to start dialysis and  she was actually in the room that day  and she said i'll give her a kidney i  was like what do i need to do where do i  start it's just amazing when people step  forward and want to help another person  like that as a transplant kidney doctor  we work with both patients with kidney  failure that need a kidney transplant  and make sure that they're healthy  enough to undergo the procedure we also  evaluate  kidney donors living kidney donors and  ensure that they're healthy enough and  that it's safe for them to be a kidney  donor so that's how i was involved in  both  requita's care and  christina as a living kidney donor  we went in for surgery they put you out  immediately you feel no pain no  anxiousness anything  when you come out of  the surgery they monitor you  for a little while just to make sure  that you're you're doing good and you're  responding like you're supposed to and  then you go into the recovery room  and then another phenomenal doctor that  prescribes all your medicines  make sure that you have the right dosage  and the right medication that is good  for you unfortunately every day 11 or 12  people die waiting for a kidney  transplant  living kidney donation offers really  quite a bit of hope for for these  patients and is really life changing for  them  living kidney donation allows us to  oftentimes do the transplant sooner  and the outcomes are usually better than  compared to a deceased donor and now i  take  a few medications in the morning and a  few at night and i can do everything  with my kids  and they see a happy healthier  more energized mom it's really a  life-changing event for the recipient  it tends to double their life expectancy  and usually they have a much improved  quality of life if not a near normal  life the thought of  my kidney making her feel amazing and  look so much better and healthy and to  be there for her family for  years and years and years to come  that's you know for me someone asked me  do i feel like i did before  i was sick  which has been 15 years so  my whole adult life  i've had this illness  and i don't know what that felt like but  it's hard  i feel great  thank you to intermountain healthcare  for sponsoring that segment for more  information on the benefits of living  donor transplants and the national  kidney registry head to registerme.org
 

